ility networks grow, and the number of permanently inhabited bases increases to inculde all areas of interest on the Moon. The book stresses that the envisioned \"Planet Moon Project\" will link the technological and cultural expertise of humanity to the virtually limitless resources of space. From that beginning, the people of the Earth reap substatntial benefits from space, and the human species will evolve into a spacefaring civilization.\nL226: For example, the Moon WILL be the next principal focus of space exploration and development, and the south polar region is increasingly likely as the first site of permanent lunar activities, as the authors predicted. Major events in space that have taken place since the publication of the 1^{st} edition of the Moonbook.\nL227: ## Accessibility information\nL228: \nL229: Accessibility information for this book is coming soon. We're working to make it available as quickly as possible. Thank you for your patience.\nL230: ## Bibliographic information\nL231: \nL232:   * Book title: The Moon\nL233: \nL234:   * Book subtitle: Resources, Future Development and Settlement\nL235: \nL236:   * Authors: David G. Schrunk, Burton L. Sharpe, Bonnie L. Cooper, Madhu Thangavelu\nL237: \nL238:   * Series title: 【28†Springer Praxis Books】\nL239: \nL240:   * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-73982-3\nL241: \nL242:   * Publisher: Praxis New York, NY\nL243: \nL244:   * eBook packages: 【55†Physics and Astronomy】, 【56†Physics and Astronomy (R0)】\nL245: \nL246:   * Copyright information: Praxis 2007\nL247:   * Softcover ISBN: 978-0-387-36055-3 Published: 04 October 2007\nL248: \nL249:   * eBook ISBN: 978-0-387-73982-3 Published: 27 November 2007\nL250: \nL251:   * Series ISSN: 2945-7475\nL252: \nL253:   * Series E-ISSN: 2945-7483\nL254: \nL255:   * Edition number: 2\nL256: \nL257:   * Number of pages: XLVI, 261\nL258: \nL259:   * Additional information: Jointly published with Praxis Publishing\nL260:   * Topics: 【57†Popular Science in Astron